NAME
    test, [ - evaluate conditional expressions

SYNOPSIS
    test EXPR
    [ EXPR ]

DESCRIPTION
    Evaluates a conditional expression and reports the result solely via
    exit status. '[' is an alias requiring a final ']' argument.

    Supported expressions:

    VALUE               True if VALUE is non-empty.
    -z VALUE            True if VALUE is empty.
    -n VALUE            True if VALUE is non-empty.
    -f PATH             True if PATH resolves inside the sandbox and is
                        a regular file. Paths outside the sandbox
                        evaluate to false.
    -d PATH             True if PATH resolves inside the sandbox and is
                        a directory. Paths outside the sandbox evaluate
                        to false.
    LHS = RHS           True if strings LHS and RHS are equal.
    LHS == RHS          True if strings LHS and RHS are equal.
    LHS != RHS          True if strings LHS and RHS differ.
    LHS -eq RHS         True if 64-bit integers LHS and RHS are equal.
    LHS -ne RHS         True if integers LHS and RHS differ.
    LHS -lt RHS         True if integer LHS is less than RHS.
    LHS -le RHS         True if integer LHS is less than or equal to RHS.
    LHS -gt RHS         True if integer LHS is greater than RHS.
    LHS -ge RHS         True if integer LHS is greater than or equal to
                        RHS.
    ! EXPR              Logical negation of EXPR.

    Compound operators (-a, -o, parentheses) are not supported; combine
    tests with shell && and || operators instead.

EXAMPLES
    if [ -f /Logs/run.log ]; then echo found; fi
    if test "$X" -eq 5; then echo five; fi
    [ "$A" == "$B" ] && [ -d /Saves ]

EXIT STATUS
    0   Expression evaluated to true.
    1   Expression evaluated to false, or expression syntax/type error.

SEE ALSO
    [(1), if(1), while(1), true(1), false(1)
